    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "So you're saying it's a \"valid renegotiation\"? That sounds like a massive stretch. If the people don't actually know the extent of the surveillance or how their data is being used, they can't possibly be \"consenting\" to it. How can you call it a valid reinterpretation of the social contract when the fundamental mechanism of the contract—informed consent—is being bypassed by the state itself? It seems like you're almost making an excuse for the state to ignore the very principles you're supposed to be analyzing.",
